#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3326bfbc-602b-5b97-a232-910eb4540d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3326bfbc-602b-5b97-a232-910eb4540d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441399} :answer-field/id #uuid "78edd9c5-7539-5d54-8eb1-437fb325e3d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1fd71a3-f959-5632-b94a-c770e9c6d2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1fd71a3-f959-5632-b94a-c770e9c6d2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441414} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e3b777-9db3-51a5-ab92-8bddd54cd065" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a25c3b40-7f3b-57d8-a341-b7f66aac34c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-251826" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e95f9087b5b607f2d33d49c0f5db123eede589e53adc94747f3a33c1fe68f90.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-8,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,1),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+1)^{2} & =4p(-6+8) \\\\ 4 & =4p(2) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm 4-1 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $3$ and  $-5.$"}]}
